# THIS INSTANCE'S BENCH, DECLARED. Checked in, no secret in it — the bench mints its own (.forge-bench/).
#
# ★ IB-3 — THE DEMO'S DECLARATION OF THE FORGE BENCH KIT. Every other file under bench/ is the kit, byte for
# byte (bench-kit.lock says from which commit; bin/bench-kit.guard.mjs holds it). THIS file is the seam: what
# differs from the kit's own copy is marked ★ demo, and the end of the file says what the demo's compose needs
# that the kit does not derive. What the kit cannot express for this box is listed in README §2, "What the kit
# bench does not cover" — and `bin/box-up.sh` keeps doing it.
#
# It is the ONLY configuration file the bench reads: production's `.env` is never read, because it names your
# public hostnames, your bucket and your CDN. A value set in the SHELL wins over this file, which is how one
# developer moves their own bench without editing it:
#
#   COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=shop-b FORGE_BENCH_PORT_BLOCK=71 bash bench/up.sh

# --- the fronts ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One line per front, and the bench assumes nothing about any of them. Each is one of:
#   image          the one forge.lock pins (the reference implementation)
#   build:<dir>    built from a directory of this repository with a Dockerfile in it (your fork)
#   external:<p>   a process you run on this machine on port <p>, listening on 0.0.0.0 (not 127.0.0.1: the edge
#                  is a container and reaches your machine through its bridge). The bench routes the door to it
#                  and writes the kernel's address into .forge-bench/fronts.env for it to read.
#                  `external:<host>:<p>` points at another host.
#   none           no front; its door answers 503 saying so
# A front beyond these three (a totem, a second vitrine) is a service in a compose file of yours, named in
# FORGE_BENCH_COMPOSE_FILES below — the same way it reaches production.
FORGE_BENCH_STOREFRONT=image
FORGE_BENCH_CHECKOUT=image
FORGE_BENCH_ADMIN=image

# --- where it lives -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two digits, 11–99: every door lands on NNxx — 00 shop · 01 admin · 04 mailbox · 05 kernel. Change it (or set
# it in the shell) for a second bench on the same machine, together with COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME.
FORGE_BENCH_PORT_BLOCK=82
# Where a browser reaches it. Born on localhost; `bash bench/promote.sh <host or IP | tailnet | localhost>` moves
# it (written to .forge-bench/address.env, this copy only — not here, so one developer's address never lands in
# git). Promoted, the bench adds two https doors on this block: NN43 shop · NN41 admin. Declare a value here only
# if EVERY bench of this repository should be born elsewhere.
# FORGE_BENCH_ADDRESS=localhost
# For `tailnet`: this machine's MagicDNS name (unset → read from `tailscale status`, only for that destination)
# and, optionally, its tailnet IP, claimed too so a device with MagicDNS off reaches the shop.
# FORGE_TAILNET_HOST=
# FORGE_TAILNET_IP=
# For a host or IP: certificates of your own instead of the bench's local CA (bench/certs/README.md), and the
# interface the two https doors listen on (every interface by default; narrow it to one VPN's address).
# FORGE_BENCH_TLS_CERT_DIR=./bench/certs
# FORGE_BENCH_TLS_BIND=0.0.0.0
# Unset → compose's own default: the directory's name. Two copies of this repository with the same directory
# name need one each; the bench refuses a project another copy owns.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=

# --- what it is born with -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The tenant, its first store and the first operator, created by the kernel's own provisioning one-shot — the
# same `provision-ref` production runs at step 4. No catalogue: that is the seed hook's job, below.
# ★ demo: the FIRST tenant of seed/box.json and its bootstrap store. ONE tenant: the kit provisions exactly one,
# and its admin serves exactly one (bench/up.sh refuses an empty FORGE_ADMIN_TENANT). forgecafe — the café,
# its fork and the totem — is born by `bash bin/box-up.sh` only (README §2).
FORGE_REF_TENANT=forgeco
FORGE_REF_STORE_HANDLE=forge
FORGE_REF_STORE_NAME=Forge
FORGE_REF_LOCALE=pt-BR
FORGE_REF_TIMEZONE=America/Sao_Paulo
# The operator invited at birth. Fictitious on purpose: the code arrives in the bench's own mailbox, and
# nothing is ever sent off this machine.
FORGE_ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL=operator@bench.example.test
FORGE_ADMIN_SEED_NAME=Bench operator

# THE SEED HOOK: a command of this repository, run after the box is up, to give the bench the data you want —
# your dataset, an importer, a few products. Empty → the bench is born with the store and nothing in it.
# It runs on this machine with: FORGE_BENCH_KERNEL_URL (the kernel), FORGE_BENCH_TENANT, FORGE_BENCH_STORE_ID,
# and FORGE_BENCH_OPERATOR_TOKEN_FILE (a file holding the operator credential — read it, never print it). It
# runs on EVERY `up`, so make it idempotent. bench/seed.example.sh is a working example.
# ★ demo: the box's own seed, through the port — seed-box (the second store, the settings), the address claim
# and the CURATED catalogue, then the doors opened anonymously (step 14-bis). The massive catalogue (23 min) only
# when FORGE_DEMO_BENCH_DATASET=1 is in the shell; see the hook's header.
FORGE_BENCH_SEED_HOOK=bash bench-demo/seed-hook.sh
# Declared here (empty) so the SHELL can say it: a name this file does not declare is dropped by the kit.
FORGE_DEMO_BENCH_DATASET=

# --- your apps ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The same two lines as .env.example: the kernel loads ./extensions, exactly as in production.
FORGE_EXTENSIONS_DIR=/app/extensions
FORGE_EXTENSIONS_HOST_DIR=./extensions

# Extra compose files merged after the bench's own overlay (relative to this directory, space-separated).
# `compose.override.yml` is merged by itself when it exists.
# ★ demo: what this compose needs on a kit bench (the origins, the mailbox's profile, the café's services off) —
# bench-demo/compose.yml says each one and why.
FORGE_BENCH_COMPOSE_FILES=bench-demo/compose.yml

# A fixed bench value, so the kernel can bust the storefront's cache when something a shopper sees changes.
# It guards a route that drops caches on a box on your own machine; production's comes from env-source.sh.
FORGE_REVALIDATE_SECRET=bench-revalidate-secret
# A bench seeds through the anonymous door too; production's 30 per 60 s would throttle it.
FORGE_PUBLIC_WRITE_LIMIT_PER_IP=100000

# --- ★ demo: what THIS compose.yml asks for that the kit does not derive ---------------------------------------
# The kit was written against the TEMPLATE's compose.yml; this box's grew its own required names. Each value below
# exists only so compose can READ the base file (`${NAME:?…}` refuses before any overlay is merged); the value a
# container actually gets is the kit's, set by bench/compose.bench.yml or bench-demo/compose.yml. Measured
# 2026-10-10: without them `bash bench/compose.sh config` dies on the first, POSTGRES_PASSWORD.
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD     the kit's postgres takes FORGE_BENCH_POSTGRES_PASSWORD (minted, .forge-bench/secrets.env)
#   FORGE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN   every service re-pointed at FORGE_BENCH_ORIGIN in bench-demo/compose.yml
#   FORGE_TOTEM_STORE_ID  the totem is not started on a kit bench (no counter store: forgecafe is not born)
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=read-by-compose-only-the-bench-mints-its-own
FORGE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://read-by-compose-only.invalid
FORGE_TOTEM_STORE_ID=sto_PENDING_SEED
# The bench's edge, the same file `bin/box-up.sh` uses locally. The production Caddyfile DOES validate with the kit's
# `http://:80` (caddy validate, 2026-10-10) — but on ONE localhost origin the second store (outlet) is path-scoped,
# and only caddy/Caddyfile.local routes its `/s/<id>/checkout` to the checkout and reads caddy/extra-local/.
FORGE_CADDYFILE=./caddy/Caddyfile.local
# The dataset the massive catalogue reads (only with FORGE_DEMO_BENCH_DATASET=1 — the hook's step 9): the same
# three values .env.example declares. The photo cache is this bench's own volume (`<project>_seed_photos`),
# and `bash bench/down.sh -v` drops it: FORGE_SEED_PHOTOS_VOLUME (DX-I3's shared cache) is NOT declared here on
# purpose — the kit's `down -v` removes a volume of that name whichever bench created it (measured 2026-10-10,
# compose 5.3.1), so declaring it would let one bench's `down -v` empty every bench's cache.
FORGE_SEED_DATASET_HOST_DIR=./seed/dataset
FORGE_SEED_DATASET_DIR=/app/seed-dataset
FORGE_SEED_PHOTOS_DIR=/data/seed-photos
